  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 8,
    "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Complex Exponential",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert e^(j $\\omega$_0 t) into the transform pair $X(j \\omega) = 2 pi \\delta(\\omega - omega_0) by recognizing it as a single frequency component shifted away from zero frequency$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Complex Exponential",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fourier Transform of a Complex Exponential",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A pure complex exponential $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ is a single rotating frequency component, not a mixture of frequencies. In the Fourier transform, that means its content is concentrated at one angular frequency instead of spread across the spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega_0 t} \\leftrightarrow 2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-\\omega_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read that pair as: the time signal $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ becomes a single impulse at $\\omega=\\omega_0$. The transform is not a broad curve; it is one spectral line located exactly where the exponential rotates.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-j\\omega_0 t} \\leftrightarrow 2\\pi\\delta(\\omega+\\omega_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the sign in the exponent controls which side of zero the impulse sits on: positive $\\omega_0$ places the impulse to the right of zero frequency, and negative $\\omega_0$ places it to the left. The examples that follow will reuse this same pair first at concrete positive and negative frequencies, and then with a scale factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Complex Exponential",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Converting e^(j $\\omega$_0 t) to a Shifted Frequency Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=e^{j\\omega_0 t}$, find $X(j\\omega)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that $x(t)=e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ is a pure complex exponential, so it contains one angular frequency only. That means its Fourier transform is not spread across many frequencies; it collapses to a single shifted impulse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega_0 t} \\leftrightarrow 2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-\\omega_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use that standard pair directly: a tone at $\\omega_0$ becomes one impulse centered at the same angular frequency. There is no extra algebra here beyond recognizing the signal as the single-frequency case from the introduction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j\\omega_0 t} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; X(j\\omega)=2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-\\omega_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The shift stays inside the impulse argument as $\\omega-\\omega_0$, so the spectral line is displaced from zero by exactly $\\omega_0$. The $2\\pi$ factor stays unchanged, and this is the form the later examples will specialize with concrete frequencies and scaling.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Complex Exponential",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Placing the Spectral Impulse at a Positive Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the Fourier transform of $x(t)=e^{j4t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that $x(t)=e^{j4t}$ is still a pure complex exponential, but now the angular frequency is the positive number $4$. So the transform should be the same single-impulse pattern, just centered at a positive frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j4t} \\leftrightarrow 2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the result directly: the spectral impulse sits at $\\omega=4$, which is to the right of zero frequency. Nothing about the transform pair changes except the concrete value of $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j4t} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; X(j\\omega)=2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The substitution is the whole move: replace $\\omega_0$ by $4$, keep the $2\\pi$ factor, and keep the impulse shift as $\\omega-4$. That is the positive-frequency case the later examples will contrast with a negative frequency and with a scale factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Complex Exponential",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Placing the Spectral Impulse at a Negative Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the Fourier transform of $x(t)=e^{-j5t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that $x(t)=e^{-j5t}$ is still a pure complex exponential, but now the angular frequency is negative. So the transform is still a single spectral impulse; it just moves to the left of zero frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0=-5 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; X(j\\omega)=2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-\\omega_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $\\omega_0=-5$ into the shift rule. That gives $\\delta(\\omega-(-5))$, which simplifies to $\\delta(\\omega+5)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-j5t} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; X(j\\omega)=2\\pi\\delta(\\omega+5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result confirms the negative-frequency placement: the impulse sits at $\\omega=-5$, not at a positive frequency. The only change from the positive-frequency case is the sign inside the $\\delta$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Complex Exponential",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Scaled Complex Exponential",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the Fourier transform of $x(t)=3e^{j2t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that $x(t)=3e^{j2t}$ is the same single-frequency complex exponential as before, but with a scalar factor of $3$ in front. That means the impulse stays at the same frequency and only its weight changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j2t} \\leftrightarrow 2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-2), \\qquad 3x(t) \\leftrightarrow 3X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply linearity to carry the factor of $3$ through the transform pair. The frequency location does not move, so the impulse remains at $\\omega=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=3e^{j2t} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; X(j\\omega)=3\\cdot 2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-2)=6\\pi\\delta(\\omega-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result shows the only effect of the scale factor: it multiplies the impulse amplitude from $2\\pi$ to $6\\pi$. The spectral impulse is still centered at $\\omega=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
